1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,600
Welcome to Two-Minute Training Tuesday.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:08,400
Last week this drop-down changed the whole report.

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:13,400
Here's what I didn't tell you — I typed those six names in by hand.

4
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:20,200
The feature behind every drop-down lives on the Data tab.

5
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:23,300
Data Validation. Here's the basic way to build one.

6
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:26,000
Allow: List.

7
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:31,500
And for Source, just select the cells your list lives in.

8
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:38,000
That's a working drop-down, and it's how most of ours are built.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,400
Now watch it fail.

10
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:48,400
New line on the export. New cost center — Powder Line 3.

11
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,400
Back to the report. Open the drop-down.

12
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:04,000
Still six. The export knows about Powder Line 3. The drop-down doesn't, and it will never tell you.

13
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:10,500
So point Source at a formula instead of at cells I typed.

14
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:16,500
SORT and UNIQUE from last week, on the cost center column.

15
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:27,500
Enter. Seven — it read the data, including the row I just added.

16
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:31,900
Same dialog. New Source.

17
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:39,000
O5, hash. This cell and everything the formula spilled into.

18
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:45,400
There it is. Powder Line 3, and I never touched the drop-down.

19
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:54,000
Next week: the Outlook folder that fills itself.
